 :new-instrument
 {:what "query_bench.cljs -- the first measurement of the query plane"
  :landed "net-kotobase/control-plane main 61d40715b"
  :why-it-had-to-be-built
  "Every latency figure for sparql/cypher/gremlin/graphql came from GET
   /health, which since 2026-08-17 returns four constants from ABOVE the router
   and before that measured the hydrate. Neither version ever ran a query. And
   all four answer 401 to a self-issued CACAO, so the only honest place to
   measure them is in-process."
  :receipt "90-docs/kotobase-performance/2026-08-18-a-query-is-one-percent.edn"}

 :the-query-answer
 {:measured {:hydrate-cpu-ms 42726.6
             :sparql-query-cpu-ms 632.8 :sparql-share 0.0148
             :cypher-query-cpu-ms  17.5 :cypher-share 0.0004
             :sparql-enrich-cpu-ms 12.3 :cypher-enrich-cpu-ms 0.7}
  :statement
  "A query is 0.04-1.5% of a request. The hydrate is the rest. Against
   production's 2,058 cpu-ms per served read the whole query is ~30 ms, so
   optimising the query engine to ZERO is worth about $0.62 per million and
   cannot be worth more."
  :so-the-answer-to-the-question-asked
  "**There is nothing to optimise in the query plane.** The cost of a query on
   kotobase.net is the cost of the hydrate that precedes it, and every
   remaining item on the roadmap is about the hydrate."
  :two-new-hypotheses-both-near-zero
  {:h9-query-engine {:saves 0.0 :basis :measured-and-refuted-for-this-shape}
   :h10-query-memo {:saves 0.0 :basis :measured
                    :note "it WORKS -- sparql 632.8 -> 82.8, 7.6x -- and that is
                           1.3% of a request, collected only on a second query
                           in a warm isolate, which 4 requests in 10 are."}}
  :enrich-was-the-surprise
  "`query/enrich` runs on every request and rebuilds `collections`, the GraphQL
   resolvers and the memo version from the whole seed. It reads like a
   per-request O(docs) walk worth attacking. Measured: 0.7-12.3 cpu-ms. Recorded
   because it is exactly the shape that attracts optimisation on inspection."}

